  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Algerian chaabi (\"popular\") urban-folk tradition of Algiers and broader Algerian coastal region, emerging in 1920s-1930s Algiers Casbah as working-class popular reinterpretation of older malhun classical-Arabic poetic tradition, with simplified modal structures and accessible Algerian-Arabic dialect lyrics. Founding figure El Hadj M'Hamed El Anka codified the genre, establishing instrumentation of oud, mandole, banjo (introduced through American jazz exposure), darbuka, and tar frame-drum. Repertoire spans poetic-philosophical reflection (qsida), urban-life observation, and post-1962 independence cultural-pride themes.",
